muzzle Library Exception, version 1.0

(an additional permission under section 7 of the GNU General Public
License, version 3, modeled on the wolf Runtime Library Exception and
the GCC Runtime Library Exception)

You have permission to propagate a work of Target Code formed by
combining the Library with Independent Modules, even if such
propagation would otherwise violate the terms of GPLv3, provided that
the Independent Modules were not derived from or based on the Library.
You may then convey such a combination under terms of your choice,
consistent with the licensing of the Independent Modules.

Definitions: "Library" means muzzle (its source, its headers, and the
archives built from them), or a work based on it. "Independent Modules"
means modules, in any language and built by any compiler, that are not
derived from or based on the Library; a program that only includes the
Library's headers and calls the interfaces they declare is not derived
from the Library by doing so. "Target Code" means object code,
executables, or intermediate forms produced by compiling and linking.

In plain words: a program you link against muzzle, statically or
dynamically, is yours, under any license you choose. Changes to muzzle
itself stay under the GPL. The exception exists so a C library's
copyleft never reaches through the linker into the programs it serves.
